 "content": {
  "subject": "Basic Electrical Engineering",
  "topic": "Energy Consumption and the Kilowatt-hour Concept",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Does ten bulbs for one hour consume more power than one for ten?",
  "question": "A 100-watt bulb runs for 10 hours, while ten 100-watt bulbs run for 1 hour. How does the total electrical energy consumed in each case compare?",
  "options": [
   "Both consume equal energy",
   "One bulb consumes ten times more",
   "Ten bulbs consume ten times more",
   "One bulb consumes one hundred times more"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 0,
  "answerLine": "Both cases consume exactly the same energy.",
  "explanation": [
   "Electrical energy is simply defined as the product of total electrical power consumed and operating time.",
   "In standard engineering practice, the commercial unit of billing is the kilowatt-hour, equivalent to one Board of Trade unit.",
   "For the first case, a single one-hundred-watt lamp operates steadily for a total duration of ten hours.",
   "Multiplying one hundred watts by ten hours gives one thousand watt-hours, which equals exactly one kilowatt-hour."
  ],
  "funFact": "One kilowatt-hour equals 3.6 megajoules, which is enough energy to boil about ten litres of room-temperature water.",
  "outro": "Remember that energy integrates power over time, so share this quiz and follow for more engineering problems!",
